#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""mac-port-scan: a REPORT of the constructs in shell files that are GNU-only, Linux-only or bash 4+, with the BSD or bash 3.2 replacement (M-1a TOOLING; bob).

  python3 -I scripts/mac-port-scan [--format text|json] [--strict] [FILE ...]
  python3 -I scripts/mac-port-scan --list-rules
  python3 -I scripts/mac-port-scan --help

LIMIT (kai, row efcb10fe), verbatim: catches the known 3.2 differences; NOT the real /bin/bash of a Mac, Apple's 3.2.57 with its own patches; proves nothing about what is not listed

With no FILE it reads deploy/install.sh and deploy/db-roles.sh of this checkout. It only READS: it executes nothing, writes nothing, changes nothing. A REPORT: it exits 0 with
findings; --strict exits 1 on an error-level finding; 2 is a refusal (usage, an unreadable file). Every rule below is held by a known-bad and a known-good line in
scripts/mac-port-fixtures/rules.txt, and the rules and their replacements are the author's reading of documentation and of the BSD manuals: where it is not sure it says ASK-A-MAC.

What a rule never sees: comments, here-document bodies and the text of quoted strings are blanked by a small lexer first (a `sed -i` in an error message is not a finding), but
$(...), backticks, ${...} words and arithmetic expansions inside double quotes and unquoted here-documents ARE scanned. A command is found where a command is (after ; && || | ( { then do ... and behind the
wrappers sudo env command xargs timeout nice nohup exec time and find -exec), not where it is only an argument. KNOWN LIMITS: a command named through a variable or an alias
is not seen, nor is an eval or sh -c of a string built at run time; a rule that reads the text of a word cannot see a word built at run time. A fixed script handed to another shell as text (sh -c 'script', eval, trap 'handler' SIGNAL, three levels deep) IS scanned and reported on the line of the outer command.
A line may be allowed with `# portable: ok <reason>` in a real comment (the Linux-only branch of an installer): it is listed as a NOTE with its reason. A marker without a reason is
itself an error (MP-900) and allows nothing.
Output (text): the REPORT line, then `file:line: severity rule message; use: replacement` per finding in file and line order, `NOTE file:line: rule allowed: reason`, then the summary line.
"""
import json
import os
import re
import sys

PROG = 'mac-port-scan'
LIMIT = "catches the known 3.2 differences; NOT the real /bin/bash of a Mac, Apple's 3.2.57 with its own patches; proves nothing about what is not listed"
REPORT_LINE = ("REPORT from documentation and the author's reading of the BSD manuals; it is not a proof that anything else is portable to macOS "
               "(a command named through a variable is not seen; see --help)")

# ---- the rule table: id, severity, title, replacement -------------------------------------------------------

